micro_lesson.content:
Two pointers usually yield O(n) time, but hidden costs can appear. A frequent runtime mistake is resetting one pointer inside the loop, turning an O(n) algorithm into O(n²). Another issue is using two pointers on a data structure that does not support O(1) random access, such as a linked list without a tail pointer. Consider this inefficient pattern:

```python
def count_pairs(nums, target):
    count = 0
    for i in range(len(nums)):
        left = i + 1
        right = len(nums) - 1
        while left < right:
            if nums[left] + nums[right] == target:
                count += 1
                left += 1
                right -= 1
            elif nums[left] + nums[right] < target:
                left += 1
            else:
                right -= 1
    return count
```

Here, the outer loop repeats the two-pointer scan for every starting index, causing quadratic time. Recognize when a single two-pointer pass suffices instead of nested traversal.

